  "query_preview": "..An impressive convective outburst on Monday evening \nhas led to the development of a large MCS over northern Mexico and \nsoutheastern Arizona this morning. The MCS is largely decaying, but \nstill contains decent convective elements. Because of this MCS, a \nfairly well-defined MCV is also expected to develop this morning. \nThis MCV will then track across southern Arizona today and up \nthrough the Lower Colorado River tonight/early Wednesday.\n\nThis type of thing ca wreak complete havoc on the forecast. Now, we \nmust factor in a large amount of debris cloud, how will that inhibit \nconvection today? It will also drive higher dew points northward for \nthe remainder of the morning. The entire time this is happening, the \nmid-level high to our east temporarily weakens a bit, which will \npotentially destabilize the atmosphere, when compared to Monday.\n\nAll these factors could easily lead to a more active day today, but \nwe have rain-cooled air and debris cloud to deal with. Much of the \ndebris cloud and potential morning showers will affect the southern \nportions of our CWA, but the northern zones may very well get that \ncherished, sunny start.\n\nThe best guess at this time will be for convective showers to move \ninto the CWA this morning, mainly south of I-40 and into parts of \nYavapai County. Expect a delayed start elsewhere. Conditions north \nof I-40, all the way up to the Kaibab, may become quite active by \nafternoon, due to the factors mentioned above and the potential for \na warmer and sunnier start.\n\nAfter today, the high begins to move westward and strengthen, This \nslowly introduces drier, mid-level air and subsidence, activity will \nslow down. We don't expect a total stop, but it will be slow through \nthe weekend. The high may return to a more favorable location by \nnext week, with easterly steering flow returning, but much can \nchange in a week and we will see.\n\nAdditionally, once the high relocates and strengthens, along with \nthe Monsoon pause/slow down, temperatures will soar. We expect many \nareas to reach the warmest readings so far this year. The various \nheat products that have been issued are appropriate.",
